        {
          "path": "_m/w/0/z/q/snake-temple-wp/see-the-snakes-now-12255012284.jpg",
          "filename": "See the snakes now? (12255012284).jpg",
          "caption": "I finally did. And that's the main sanctum sanctorum of the temple. And no, they do not worship snakes here. Instead they honour Chor Soo Kong, a do gooder saint and healer, who used to even give shelter to snakes from the nearby forest. Locals claim that the snakes come here of their own accord. Do you see the snakes now? They are those animals coiled on the metal rings on either side of the main sanctum. (Georgetown, Penang, Malaysia, Nov. 2013)",
          "alt": "I finally did. And that's the main sanctum sanctorum of the temple. And no, they do not worship snakes here. Instead they honour Chor Soo Kong, a do gooder saint and healer, who used to even give shelter to snakes from the nearby forest. Locals claim that the snakes come here of their own accord. Do you see the snakes now? They are those animals coiled on the metal rings on either side of the main sanctum. (Georgetown, Penang, Malaysia, Nov. 2013)",
          "width": 4288,
          "height": 2848,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY 2.0",
          "artist": "shankar s. from Dubai, united arab emirates",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:See_the_snakes_now%3F_(12255012284).jpg",
          "sourceType": "commons-category",
          "sourceArticleId": "snake-temple-wp",
          "sourceGeohash4": "w0zq",
          "mediumPath": "_m/w/0/z/q/snake-temple-wp/see-the-snakes-now-12255012284-medium.webp",
          "smallPath": "_m/w/0/z/q/snake-temple-wp/see-the-snakes-now-12255012284-small.webp"
        },
        {
          "path": "_m/w/0/z/q/snake-temple-wp/deadly-vipers-up-there-in-the-.jpg",
          "filename": "Deadly vipers up there in the ring (12254545765).jpg",
          "caption": "A still close of the Wagler's Pit Viper atop the metal ring stand flanking the main sanctum of the Penang Snake Temple. During the afternoon heat, the snakes are so still and immobile that they appear fake. The bright, colourful markings and body colour almost give the appearance that they are made from rubber or plastic. Even the snakes' eyes remain transfixed. First-time visitors often mistake the snakes as fakes, discounting the temple as a poor and tacky tourist attraction. Rest assured the snakes are real, and there are warning signs around the temples asking visitors not to harass or pick up the snakes. (Georgetown, Penang, Malaysia, Nov. 2013)",
          "alt": "A still close of the Wagler's Pit Viper atop the metal ring stand flanking the main sanctum of the Penang Snake Temple. During the afternoon heat, the snakes are so still and immobile that they appear fake. The bright, colourful markings and body colour almost give the appearance that they are made from rubber or plastic. Even the snakes' eyes remain transfixed. First-time visitors often mistake the snakes as fakes, discounting the temple as a poor and tacky tourist attraction. Rest assured the snakes are real, and there are warning signs around the temples asking visitors not to harass or pick up the snakes. (Georgetown, Penang, Malaysia, Nov. 2013)",
          "width": 4288,
          "height": 2848,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY 2.0",
          "artist": "shankar s. from Dubai, united arab emirates",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deadly_vipers_up_there_in_the_ring_(12254545765).jpg",
          "sourceType": "commons-category",
          "sourceArticleId": "snake-temple-wp",
          "sourceGeohash4": "w0zq",
          "mediumPath": "_m/w/0/z/q/snake-temple-wp/deadly-vipers-up-there-in-the--medium.webp",
          "smallPath": "_m/w/0/z/q/snake-temple-wp/deadly-vipers-up-there-in-the--small.webp"
        },
        {
          "path": "_m/w/0/z/q/snake-temple-wp/i-looked-up-casually-and-saw-t.jpg",
          "filename": "I looked up casually and saw this (12254898016).jpg",
          "caption": "One think I learned in the Penang Snake Temple is, NEVER look up. My second shock that afternoon as I looked up casually: here was this Wagler's Pit Viper coiled around on a dead branch. I wondered what that stone like thing was hanging from the branch. This probably keeps the snakes partially drugged? (Georgetown, Penang, Malaysia, Nov. 2013)",
          "alt": "One think I learned in the Penang Snake Temple is, NEVER look up. My second shock that afternoon as I looked up casually: here was this Wagler's Pit Viper coiled around on a dead branch. I wondered what that stone like thing was hanging from the branch. This probably keeps the snakes partially drugged? (Georgetown, Penang, Malaysia, Nov. 2013)",
